        definition:
          'Gram-Schmidt is the constructive algorithm that turns any set of M waveforms into an orthonormal basis for the space they span. It processes the signals in order: the first basis function is the first signal normalised; each later basis function is formed by subtracting from the next signal its projections onto all basis functions already built, then normalising what remains. If the remainder is zero the signal was linearly dependent on its predecessors and contributes no new dimension, which is precisely how the algorithm discovers that N < M. The order of processing changes the basis obtained but never changes N or any distance between signal points.',
