        terms: ['correlator', 'integrate-and-dump', 'inner product', 'bank of correlators', 'symbol timing'],
        definition:
          'The correlation receiver implements the projection directly: it multiplies the received waveform by each basis function and integrates the product over the symbol interval, a structure usually built as a multiplier followed by an integrate-and-dump. N correlators in parallel produce the observation vector, which is then fed to the distance comparison. The structure is a literal circuit translation of the integral defining the inner product, which makes it easy to reason about and easy to get wrong in one specific way: the integrator must be reset at every symbol boundary, and the boundary must be known.',
        takeaway: 'A correlator is the inner-product integral built in hardware — and it is only correct if the integrator is dumped exactly at the symbol boundary.',
