        definition:
          'Entropy is bounded between zero and log2(K) for a K-symbol alphabet, reaching the upper bound only when all symbols are equally likely, and it is a concave function of the probability distribution. The nth extension of a discrete memoryless source treats blocks of n symbols as single super-symbols drawn from an alphabet of K^n, and its entropy is exactly n H(X) because the symbols are independent. This matters because coding blocks rather than individual symbols is how practical codes approach the entropy bound: the inefficiency of rounding a code length up to a whole number of bits is amortised over n symbols and shrinks as 1/n.',
