        terms: ['source coding theorem', 'average code length', 'code efficiency', 'lower bound', 'lossless compression'],
        definition:
          "The Shannon source coding theorem states that for a discrete memoryless source of entropy H(X), the average code length L of any uniquely decodable code satisfies L >= H(X), and codes exist with L less than H(X) + 1. Entropy is therefore both a hard floor and an achievable target, which is what makes it the right definition of information content. The theorem is an existence result: it guarantees a code exists without constructing one, and the gap of up to one bit comes from the need to round code lengths to whole numbers. Coding the nth extension shrinks that gap to 1/n.",
        takeaway: 'No lossless code beats entropy, and some code gets within one bit of it — a floor and a ceiling in one theorem.',
