        definition:
          'A prefix code is one in which no codeword is a prefix of any other, which makes decoding instantaneous: as soon as the received bits match a codeword, that codeword is complete and no lookahead is needed. Prefix codes correspond exactly to binary trees with codewords at the leaves, and a set of lengths l_k admits a prefix code if and only if the Kraft inequality sum of 2^(-l_k) <= 1 holds. The inequality has a direct interpretation: each codeword of length l claims a fraction 2^(-l) of the tree, and the claims cannot exceed the whole tree. Equality means the tree is full, with no wasted branches.',
        takeaway: 'Kraft says code lengths compete for one unit of tree; a prefix code exists exactly when the claims fit.',
