        terms: ['Huffman code', 'greedy merge', 'optimal prefix code', 'code tree construction', 'tie breaking'],
        definition:
          'Huffman coding constructs an optimal prefix code by repeatedly merging the two least probable symbols into a composite symbol whose probability is their sum, until one symbol remains. Reading the resulting binary tree from the root to each leaf yields the codewords, and the construction is provably optimal: no other prefix code has a smaller average length for the same distribution. Its weakness is granularity — code lengths must be whole numbers of bits, so a symbol of probability 0.9 that ideally deserves 0.152 bits still gets a full bit. Ties in the merge order produce different trees with identical average length.',
