        definition:
          'Mutual information is non-negative, symmetric in its two arguments, and concave in the input distribution for a fixed channel — the last property is what guarantees that maximising it over input distributions has a single global maximum rather than a landscape of local ones. It also obeys the data processing inequality: if X, Y and Z form a Markov chain, then I(X;Z) <= I(X;Y), meaning no amount of post-processing at the receiver can create information that the channel did not deliver. That inequality is the formal reason a clever decoder cannot beat channel capacity, only approach it.',
        takeaway: 'Concavity makes capacity computable; the data processing inequality makes it unbeatable by any amount of receiver cleverness.',
