          'The Information Capacity Law states that a band-limited AWGN channel of bandwidth B hertz and signal-to-noise ratio S/N has capacity C = B log2(1 + S/N) bits per second. It ties together the three resources a communication engineer trades between — bandwidth, power and reliability — in a single expression, and it is the continuous-channel counterpart of the discrete capacity results. Capacity grows only logarithmically with power, so doubling the transmit power adds barely one bit per hertz at high SNR, while it grows linearly with bandwidth, which is why spectrum is the more valuable of the two resources.',
