        definition:
          'A code is systematic when the k message bits appear unchanged in fixed positions of the codeword, with the remaining n-k positions holding parity bits computed from them. Every linear block code has a systematic equivalent reachable by row operations on G, and the equivalent has identical distance properties, so nothing is lost by insisting on it. The practical advantages are concrete: the encoder computes only the parity bits rather than the whole codeword, the receiver can read out the message without decoding when the channel is clean, and a decoder failure still leaves a usable if uncertain message.',
