        definition:
          'The syndrome of a received vector r is s = r H transpose, an (n-k)-bit vector. Writing r = c + e where c is the transmitted codeword and e the error pattern, and using cH transpose = 0, gives s = eH transpose: the syndrome depends only on the error pattern and not at all on which codeword was sent. That single property is what makes syndrome decoding possible, because it reduces a search over 2^k codewords to a lookup over 2^(n-k) syndromes. A zero syndrome means no detectable error; a non-zero syndrome identifies the coset the error lies in, though not the error uniquely.',
