        definition:
          'The Hamming distance between two codewords is the number of positions in which they differ, and the minimum distance d_min of a code is the smallest such distance over all pairs. For a linear code, the difference of two codewords is itself a codeword, so d_min equals the minimum Hamming weight over all non-zero codewords — a search over 2^k - 1 words instead of over all pairs. This single number determines everything about the code strength: how many errors it can detect, how many it can correct, and whether a given error pattern will be caught or slip through.',
        takeaway: 'For a linear code, minimum distance equals minimum weight, and that one number fixes all detection and correction guarantees.',
