        terms: ['Hamming code', 'perfect code', 'single error correcting', 'column construction', 'syndrome as address'],
        definition:
          'A Hamming code is a (2^m - 1, 2^m - 1 - m) code whose parity check matrix has as its columns all 2^m - 1 non-zero binary m-tuples, each appearing exactly once. Because every column is distinct and non-zero, no two columns sum to zero and no single column is zero, so d_min = 3 and the code corrects exactly one error. The construction has an elegant decoding consequence: the syndrome of a single error in position j is precisely column j of H, so if the columns are ordered as binary counting, the syndrome read as a binary number is the error address. Hamming codes are perfect: the decoding spheres of radius one tile the space exactly.',
