        definition:
          'Systematic cyclic encoding multiplies the message polynomial by x^(n-k) to shift it into the high-order positions, divides by g(x), and appends the remainder as the parity bits. The resulting codeword is x^(n-k)m(x) + r(x), which is divisible by g(x) by construction and carries the message unchanged in its top k positions. The hardware is a shift register of n-k stages with feedback taps at the positions of the non-zero coefficients of g(x), which performs the polynomial division as the message bits are clocked in. This is why cyclic codes dominate practice: the encoder is a handful of flip-flops and XOR gates rather than a matrix multiplier.',
        takeaway: 'Shift the message up, divide by g(x), append the remainder — and the whole operation is n-k flip-flops and a few XOR gates.',
