          'The trellis is the code tree with equal-state nodes merged, so it has exactly 2^M nodes at every time step regardless of sequence length. Each node has two outgoing branches, for input 0 and input 1, and after the first M steps each node also has two incoming branches. That constant width is the single structural fact that makes efficient decoding possible: the decoder need only keep one surviving path per state rather than one per possible input sequence, so the work per time step is fixed rather than doubling. Every valid encoder output sequence is a path through the trellis, and decoding means finding the path closest to what was received.',
