        slides: [29, 30, 31],
        terms: ['free distance', 'error event', 'asymptotic coding gain', 'path weight', 'diverge and remerge'],
        definition:
          'The free distance d_free of a convolutional code is the minimum Hamming weight of any non-zero path that diverges from the all-zero path and later remerges with it. It replaces minimum distance as the figure of merit because there are no blocks, and it governs performance the same way: the asymptotic coding gain in decibels is 10 log10(R d_free) for hard decisions, with an extra 3 dB available for soft decisions. An error event is one such diverge-and-remerge excursion, and at reasonable signal-to-noise ratios almost all errors are minimum-weight events, which is why d_free dominates.',
        takeaway: 'd_free is the weight of the cheapest wrong path that rejoins the right one; coding gain is 10 log10(R d_free).',
        visual: 'free-distance-error-event',
        visualSpec:
          'A trellis over eight time steps with the all-zero path drawn as a bold horizontal line along the top row. A second path diverges from it at step 2, wanders through the lower states for three steps, and remerges at step 5, drawn in a contrasting colour. The output weight of each branch on the diverging path is labelled, and the labels are summed in a running total beside the path, reaching 5 at the remerge point, boxed and labelled "d_free = 5". A side card gives the coding gain calculation for rate 1/2 and d_free 5, both hard and soft. On reveal: the trellis grid appears, then the all-zero path draws bold left to right, then the diverging path draws branch by branch with each branch weight label appearing as it is drawn, then the running sum counts up beside it, then the remerge point pulses and the total boxes, then the side card slides in with its two gain figures.',
