        topic: 'The Viterbi algorithm: add, compare, select',
        slides: [35, 36, 37],
        terms: ['Viterbi algorithm', 'branch metric', 'path metric', 'survivor path', 'add-compare-select'],
        definition:
          'The Viterbi algorithm walks the trellis one time step at a time, maintaining for each state the best path reaching it and that path accumulated metric. At each step and for each state it adds the branch metric to the metric of each incoming path, compares the two candidates, and selects the smaller, discarding the loser permanently. Discarding is justified because any path continuing from the loser can be improved by swapping in the winner, so the loser can never be part of the final best path. The work per time step is 2^M add-compare-select operations, independent of how long the sequence is.',
        takeaway: 'Add the branch metric, compare the two candidates, select the better, discard the other forever — 2^M times per step.',
